gapH(){return"Cervix"},
gajO(){return"Measure immediately after waking, before getting up and before any activity such as speaking, sitting or drinking. Always at about the same time each morning and after sufficient sleep. Interruptions such as going to bed late, frequent night awakenings, alcohol or illness are marked as measurement disturbances. The measurement takes 5 minutes (vaginal or rectal) or 8 minutes (oral). Temperature is entered in steps of 0.05 K \u2014 that is precise enough, because thermometers, even digital ones showing two decimal places, typically measure to an accuracy of only 0.1 \xb0C to 0.05 \xb0C: values from 36.68 to 36.72 become 36.70, and values from 36.73 to 36.77 become 36.75."},
gajJ(){return"Observe your bleeding and its strength day by day. The first day of serious bleeding after a high temperature is the first cycle day and is marked as the cycle start with the cycle-start switch."},
gajL(){return"Observe your fertility sign: the sensation at the vaginal opening and the visible mucus. The observation is entered through the signs t, \xd8, f, S and A; the quality qualifier belongs to the S sign only."},
gajK(){return"The cervix is felt daily with your own finger (clean hands, short nails) \u2014 best at the same time each day and in the same posture. Position (low to unreachable), opening and firmness are recorded."},
gajN(){return"Recorded is sexual intercourse with ejaculation into the vagina. The time of day (morning, midday, evening) is noted too because it is fertility-relevant: sperm can survive several days, so intercourse before the fertile time can lead to pregnancy. Several times of day can be selected together for the same day."},
gajM(){return"Two pain observations: cyclical breast pain (the typical tension-type pain of the second half of the cycle) and mittelschmerz, the possible ovulation pain around mid-cycle."},
gajP(){return"Remove temperature"},
gajQ(){return"Temperature"},
